                    Method called by Garbage Collector

Runs before object is removed from Heap
Called at most once
Execution not guaranteed
Cannot predict when it runs
Used for cleanup (old practice)
Deprecated (avoid using)
Prefer finally / try-with-resources
Memory line to remember:
finalize() = unreliable last chance cleanup
Prefer try-with-resources not finalize.
finalize() is a method of Object class.
It is called by the Garbage Collector before an object is destroyed.
Used for cleanup work (like closing resources, releasing memory).
Syntax:
protected void finalize() throws Throwable

It is NOT guaranteed to run (depends on GC).
You cannot control when it executes.
It runs only once per object (even if GC runs multiple times).
It is deprecated from Java 9 onward ❌
Replaced by better alternatives like:
try-with-resources
Explicit cleanup methods
Using finalize() is bad practice now (slow + unpredictable).
🎯 Interview One-liner:
“finalize() is a cleanup method called by GC before object destruction, but it is deprecated and unreliable.”
